===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|kĭl}}, {{IPA|en|/kɪl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-kill.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪl|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|killen}}, {{m|enm|kyllen}}, {{m|enm|cullen|cüllen|t=to strike, beat, cut}}, of obscure origin.
* Perhaps from {{inh|en|ang|*cyllan}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*kwulljan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*kwuljaną}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*gʷelH-|t=to throw, hit, hurt by throwing}}.
* Or, possibly a variant of {{inh|en|ang|cwellan|t=to kill, murder, execute}} (see {{l|en|quell}}).
* Or, from {{der|en|non|kolla|t=to hit on the head, harm}}, related to {{cog|no|kylla|t=to poll}}, {{cog|dum|kollen|t=to knock down}}, {{cog|is|kollur|t=top, head}}; see also {{l|en|coll}}, {{l|en|cole}}).

Compare also {{cog|stq|källe|t=to hurt}}, {{cog|dum|killen|t=to be cold}}, {{cog|dum|kellen|t=to kill, hurt}}, {{cog|gml|kellen}}, {{m|gml|killen|t=to ache strongly, cause one great pain}}, {{cog|gmh|kellen|t=to torment; torture}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put to [[death]]; to [[extinguish]] the [[life]] of.
#: {{ux|en|Smoking '''kills''' more people each year than alcohol and drugs combined.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|act=IV|scene=vi|page=304|passage=A Troope of Horſe with Felt: Ile put't in proofe, / And when I haue ſtolne vpon theſe Son in Lawes, / Then '''kill''', '''kill''', '''kill''', '''kill''', '''kill''', '''kill'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|III|ii|page=12|passage={{smallcaps|Stephano}}: {{w|Caliban|Monſter}}, I will '''kill''' this man : his daughter and I will be King and Queene, ſave our Graces : and ''Trinculo'' and thy ſelfe ſhall be Vice-royes : {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To render [[inoperative]].
#: {{ux|en|He '''killed''' the engine and turned off the headlights, but remained in the car, waiting.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|journal=Worlds of If|date=Feb 1965|page=33|column=1|text=He '''killed''' the motor.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1978|author=w:John Farris|title=[[w:The Fury (1978 film)|The Fury]]
|passage=''Peter'': Ask Childers if it was worth his arm.<br>''Policeman'': What did you do to his arm, Peter?<br>''Peter'': I '''killed''' it, with a machine gun.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To [[stop]], [[cease]]{{,}} or render [[void]]; to [[terminate]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022| title=The Heretic|author=Liam McIlvanney| page=244| passage=He closed the boot, walked round to the kerbside and bent to peer into the car's interior, his face pressed to the passenger window, his hands shading his eyes to '''kill''' the reflection.}}
#: {{ux|en|The editor decided to '''kill''' the story.}}
#: {{ux|en|The news that a hurricane had destroyed our beach house '''killed''' our plans to sell it.}}
#: {{ux|en|My computer wouldn't respond until I '''killed''' some of the running processes.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively|hyperbole}} To [[amaze]], [[exceed]], [[stun]]{{,}} or otherwise [[incapacitate]].
#: {{ux|en|That night, she was dressed to '''kill'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|That joke always '''kills''' me.}}
#* {{RQ:Salinger Catcher|page=23|passage=He told us we ought to think of Jesus as our buddy and all. He said ''he'' talked to Jesus all the time. Even when he was driving his car. That '''killed''' me.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively|hyperbole}} To cause great [[pain]], [[discomfort]]{{,}} or [[distress]] to; to [[hurt]].
#: {{ux|en|These tight shoes are '''killing''' my feet.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2008|month=October|author=Davy Rothbart|title=How I caught up with dad|journal=w:Men's Health|volume=23|issue=8|issn=1054-4836|page={{gbooks|VMgDAAAAMBAJ|110|killing}}|passage={{smallcaps|two laps into our first walk, my dad}} needed to sit down. His back and legs were '''killing''' him. "You'll be okay," I assured him. "You just need to shake off the rust."<br/>I gave him a couple of Advil and, after a few minutes, urged him back onto the track.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To produce feelings of [[dissatisfaction]] or [[revulsion]] in.
#: {{ux|en|It '''kills''' me to throw out three whole turkeys, but I can't get anyone to take them and they've already started to go bad.}}
#: {{ux|en|It '''kills''' me to learn how many poor people are practically starving in this country while rich moguls spend such outrageous amounts on useless luxuries.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[use up]] or to [[waste]].
#: {{ux|en|I'm just doing this to '''kill''' time.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2001|author=Jonathan Franzen|title=The Corrections
|passage=Except for the shirt, which he’d worn, and the check, which he’d cashed, and the bottle of port, which he’d '''killed''' in bed on Christmas night, the gifts from his family were still on the floor of his bedroom.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively|informal}} To exert an [[overwhelm]]ing [[effect]] on.
#: {{ux|en|Look at the amount of destruction to the enemy base. We pretty much '''killed''' their ability to retaliate anymore.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively|hyperbole}} To [[overpower]], [[overwhelm]]{{,}} or [[defeat]].
#: {{ux|en|The team had absolutely '''killed''' their traditional rivals, and the local sports bars were raucous with celebrations.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To force a company out of [[business]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|informal|hyperbolic}} To produce intense [[pain]].
#: {{ux|en|You don't ever want to get rabies. The doctor will have to give you multiple shots and they really '''kill'''.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively|informal|hyperbole|transitive}} To [[punish]] [[severely]].
#: {{ux|en|My parents are going to '''kill''' me!}}
#* {{RQ:Doyle Land of Mist|passage="Father will '''kill''' us for this."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|sports}} To strike (a ball, etc.) with such force and placement as to make a shot that is impossible to defend against, usually winning a point.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 4, 2011|author=Gareth Roberts| title=Wales 19-26 England| work=BBC| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/rugby_union/9386391.stm| passage=That close call encouraged Wales to launch another series of attacks that ended when lock Louis Deacon '''killed''' the ball illegally in the shadow of England's posts.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|sports}} To cause (a ball, etc.) to be [[out of play]], resulting in a stoppage of gameplay.
#* {{quote-journal|en| author=Nathan Schmook|url=https://www.saints.com.au/news/2015-05-10/when-jack-became-a-jet| title=Billings vs Bont| work=St Kilda Football Club| date=10 May 2015| passage=As the ball was delivered deep into St Kilda's forward line by Billings, Bontempelli had position on the goal line, with a pack forming in front of him. He decided to fly but didn't '''kill''' the ball, leaving it to spill where he had been positioned moments earlier. Jack Sinclair gratefully swooped and kicked a goal that cut the margin to five points.}}
# To succeed with an audience, especially in comedy.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Yael Kohen| title=We '''Killed''': The Rise of Women in American Comedy| url=https://books.google.com/books?id=aTtRzfNCf3UC&printsec=frontcover&dq=%22killed+it%22+comedy+%22stand-up%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwie8MftvtjLAhUE6WMKHbXOAhsQ6AEIKjAA#v=snippet&q=kill&f=false| passage=When comics fail, they "die"; when they succeed, they "'''kill'''."}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 23, 2016|author=Tim Gray| title=Chris Rock Tests Jokes at Comedy Clubs Ahead of Oscars| work=Variety| url=http://variety.com/2016/film/news/chris-rock-oscar-monologue-comedy-clubs-1201713174/| passage=You really '''killed''' it at the Comedy Store last night.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics|transitive|informal}} To cause to assume the value zero.
# {{lb|en|computing|Internet|[[IRC]]|transitive}} To [[disconnect]] (a user) involuntarily from the [[network]].
# {{lb|en|metallurgy}} To [[deadmelt]].
# {{lb|en|slang}} To [[sexually]] [[penetrate]] in a skillful way.
#* {{quote-song|1=en|year=1992|author=Ice Cube|title=It Was A Good Day|album=The Predator|text=I felt on her big fat fanny/Pulled out the jammy and killed the punanni}}
# {{lb|en|reflexive|informal}} To [[exert]] oneself to an excessive degree.
#: ''Don't '''kill yourself''' raking the leaves now; we're due for a windstorm tonight.''

